cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "The Library: The Indifference of Description"
message: "If you reference this book in academic or critical work, please cite it using these metadata."
type: software
authors:
  - given-names: Alexander
    family-names: Towell
    email: lex@metafunctor.com
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0001-6443-9897"
    affiliation: "Southern Illinois University Edwardsville"
abstract: >-
  A nonfiction book that follows the algorithmic information theory of the
  1960s to a cold conclusion and a chosen response. Generating everything is
  trivial: every possible book, every computable description, is the output of a
  short program. What is hard is picking anything out of it. Built on theorems
  (Bayes' theorem, Kraft's inequality, Solomonoff's dominance theorem), one universal
  construction (the universal prior), and a single labeled postulate (no carving
  of the totality is privileged), the book traces the consequences for continuation,
  mortality, love, memory, and the self, and arrives at the indifference of
  description and the warmth we choose to provide each other inside it. Volume III
  of the Indifference suite, after Worldlines and Multitudes.
keywords:
  - Solomonoff induction
  - universal prior
  - Kolmogorov complexity
  - algorithmic information theory
  - computable multiverse
  - eternalism
  - meaning
  - compassion
